WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:12.099
Remasters have become an accepted part of
video games in recent years. Older games are

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:16.230
given a fresh coat of paint and sometimes
even gameplay tweaks and the released on modern

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.279
systems. This year alone has seen some pretty
big remasters including BioShock, Call of

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:26.949
Duty 4: Modern Warfare, and The Elder Scrolls
V: Skyrim. But let me ask you a question.

00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:29.410
Can remasters change too much?

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.270
Because the reality is that these remasters
aren’t the same game you played all those

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:38.150
those years ago. They feature different textures,
employ modern day tech that simply wasn’t

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:42.420
possible back then. These aren’t upscale
transfers of an VHS to blu-ray, these are

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.019
Star Wars Special Editions. So what happens
when these updated versions alter too much

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:51.769
of the original art style and makes gameplay
tweaks that fundamentally make the game different?

00:00:51.769 --> 00:00:55.979
The BioShock remaster has been panned by fans
for numerous reasons including limited graphics

00:00:55.979 --> 00:01:00.590
options, changes to art and graphics, missing
audio, and somehow being even more buggy than

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:04.910
the original PC port. But I want to focus
on the changes in art, because BioShock’s

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:09.770
art style is probably its most recognizable
aspect and core to its identity.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:14.350
The changes made in the remaster are pretty
subtle and average person will probably not

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:18.710
notice them. Things like added seaweed in
the bathysphere ride. This statue of Andrew

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.120
Ryan has been remodeled, this red carpet has
been removed, and for some reason the floor

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.940
tiles in this room have been rotated. The
lighting is different in places, and the textures

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.860
are “less wet,” which is to say they are
no longer glossy and the feeling of being

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:37.630
in a damp city at the bottom of the ocean
is somewhat diminished. And the truth is a

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:43.080
lot people will not notice or even care about
this small changes, but for some, small details

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.490
like this is important because you are playing
with people's nostalgia, and odd changes like

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:52.060
the removable of this carpet for seemingly
no reason other than to make it different

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:57.080
just feels pointless. It's like when George
Lucas added this rock in front of R2-D2. It

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.430
doesn’t run the movie of anything, but just…
why?

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:05.320
But maybe carpet changes and not wet textures
feels too nitpicky for you. How about a more

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:11.390
obvious and dramatic change like the iOS mobile
remake of Final Fantasy 6? Here’s original

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.620
Final Fantasy for the Super Nintendo. Now
for the mobile version they stretched the

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:21.599
image, The backgrounds appear to be traced
over. The character sprites are completely

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:26.939
replaced. Everything is then given this blurry
smudge effect. And then the textbox is replaced

00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:32.420
with modern UI, font, and a detailed character
portrait. The final effect is a combination

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:37.989
of disparate styles that lack the cohesiveness
of the orignal pixel art. Rather then rebuild

00:02:37.989 --> 00:02:43.010
the whole game with a new artist vision, we
get this frankenstein photoshop, which mistreats

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:49.099
the original art and the artists who made
it.

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:54.430
But visuals aren’t the only thing remasters
add, some also add gameplay changes as well,

00:02:54.430 --> 00:03:00.060
which makes sense. Controls and certain mechanics
can quickly become outdated in the fast moving

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.090
world of video games, so wanting to freshen
them up for modern players can be a good idea.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:09.269
Nintendo’s been doing with with all their
remasters of the 3D Zelda games, addressing

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:13.311
complaints with the originals and fixing them
where they can. Stuff like adding a faster

00:03:13.311 --> 00:03:17.669
sail in The Wind Waker HD to cut down on the
long sailing time, or changing the “Song

00:03:17.669 --> 00:03:22.140
of Double Time” in Majora’s Mask 3DS which
likes you skip to any hour of the day, instead

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.620
of just the nearest half day. And of course
the most infamous issues of the Iron Boots

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.620
in Ocarina of Time’s Water Temple, which
in the original game you had to tediously

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.830
open the menu every time to equip and unequip,
but in the 3DS remaster was changed to a simple

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:33.769
button press. But you also need to be careful
in what we change. A video game and its mechanics

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:38.370
are tied to each other, and it's possible
to unintentionally alter the experience too

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:50.370
much.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.469
Such is the case with Doom 3’s remaster,
‘BFG Edition.” The Doom 3 has a controversial

00:03:55.469 --> 00:03:59.510
flashlight mechanic, because you could not
use it and hold a weapon and the same time.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:04.569
Many players did not care for this, leading
to the creation of the famous ducttape mod.

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:07.530
So when 2012’s remaster of Doom 3, titled
“BFG edition”, came around, the bold move

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.419
was made to made this mod official, removing
the original flashlight mechanic from the

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:19.230
game entirely. The problem is that even if
you didn’t like it, Doom 3 was designed

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:24.020
around that original flashlight. Every room
was a potential for a scare, and the comfort

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.570
you would get from being able to see with
the flashlight, came at the cost of being

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:32.050
able to shoot. The taking that away removes
a large part of the tension and suspense Doom

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:37.800
3 creates. It makes the game worse. And while
Doom 3 hasn’t aged terribly well, the flashlight

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.870
still is a unique idea that you don’t see
replicated in other games. It's a concept

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.479
whole original to Doom 3. And they just removed
it. But that's ok, because we can still load

00:04:47.479 --> 00:04:52.150
up and enjoy original Doom 3. Well that almost
wasn’t the case.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:57.220
Back when Doom 3: BFG Edition came out, Original
Doom 3 and it’s expansion were removed from

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:02.150
the steam store. No official reason was ever
said for why this happened, but if you want

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:06.300
to put on your cynic hat it could have been
because you could buy the original cheaper

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:11.101
and then just recreate all of BFG Edition’s
improvements with mods. The game eventually

00:05:11.101 --> 00:05:15.889
came back to the store a month later allow
consumers the choice in which version to buy,

00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:20.259
but this isn’t always the case. If you want
to play the horror classics Silent Hill 2

00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:27.160
and 3, you’re only choice is to buy the
HD remasters, which have been heavily criticized

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:32.419
as rushed port jobs. That iOS version of Final
Fantasy 6? That's the version that got ported

00:05:32.419 --> 00:05:33.449
to steam.

00:05:33.449 --> 00:05:37.770
And this is becoming a real issue with games
on digital stores. Publishers seem interested

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.229
in only having one version of their game on
a storefront, so if multiple version exist

00:05:42.229 --> 00:05:46.449
they tend to pick the most recent version.
A perfect example of this is the original

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:51.650
Resident Evil. Resident Evil 1 has numerous
ports and updates over the years, and even

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:56.720
a full on excellent remake. But if you still
want to play the PS1 classic it's available

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.349
digitally on the playstation store. Except
that's actually not the original release of

00:06:01.349 --> 00:06:05.860
the game. It’s the “Resident Evil: Director’s
Cut Dual Shock ver.” which was released

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:10.780
over a year after the original game and features
added content such as arranged mode, which

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.419
swaps around all the puzzle items. It's certainly
the most feature rich version which is probably

00:06:15.419 --> 00:06:20.569
why it’s the one on the store but R E D
C D S V also features a completely different

00:06:20.569 --> 00:06:23.759
soundtrack from the original, one that is
considered worse by the fans. But because

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:28.160
this is the only version that got picked to
be preserved digitally, that original soundtrack

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:55.530
will eventually be lost to time, and this
will be replaced with this.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:07:00.240
More so than any other media, games change,
especially in today’s era of updates and

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.050
patches. It’s impossible for someone starting
Destiny today to be able to play and experience

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:11.110
the original vanilla release from 2014. It's
ok and even important for games to change

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:15.699
and for devs to be allowed to make those changes,
but it's also important to respect the original

00:07:15.699 --> 00:07:20.349
versions of these games and gives players
choice in the versions they can play. When

00:07:20.349 --> 00:07:25.020
the original System Shock was finally available
digitally, Developer Nightdive studios put

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:29.099
out the “Enhanced Edition”, which updated
the graphics and improved the controls, a

00:07:29.099 --> 00:07:33.860
welcome addition because those original controls
are garbage.But it also comes with the classic

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.749
version of the game for those that want to
experience it. Nightdive is also the team

00:07:36.749 --> 00:07:37.749
responsible for the upcoming System Shock
Reboot.

00:07:37.749 --> 00:07:41.259
And then there are games that allow you to
switch between original and updated controls,

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:46.220
audio, and graphics in the game themselves.
Games like the Halo Anniversaries, and the

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:51.629
LucasArts adventure game remasters. You press
a button and suddenly you’re swapping between

00:07:51.629 --> 00:07:57.139
10 years of video game progress. Apart from
it just being a really cool gimmick, it acts

00:07:57.139 --> 00:07:59.419
as a historical showcase.

00:07:59.419 --> 00:08:04.499
And that’s what remasters should be. Celebrations
of these games and the time and history that

00:08:04.499 --> 00:08:09.939
comes with them, which also means being willing
to showing the flaws, rather than sweep them

00:08:09.939 --> 00:08:14.919
under the rug. I want remasters to be the
Blade Runner box set that comes with all 5

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:19.629
cuts, even if I will never watch that theatrical
version. Because preserving and respecting

00:08:19.629 --> 00:08:22.999
games is important, and we could be doing
a lot better job at it.

